WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.290
Smartphones, TVs, computers, iPads, and many
other modern miracles are made possible by

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.570
power-efficient LED screens.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.959
But the real impact goes well beyond our brightly
lit gadgets.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.540
Lighting accounts for 20-30% of global electricity
consumption and about 6% of

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.300
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:26.910
Given that LED bulbs use around 80% less energy,
and last 25 times longer than incandescent

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.190
lighting, they have the greatest potential
impact on energy savings globally.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:36.050
But when the first LEDs were introduced in
the 1960s, they didn’t have much use.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.860
It wasn’t until the ‘90s, when Japanese
scientists discovered the missing link needed

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.739
to complete the color spectrum: the blue LED.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:50.800
Here’s how the color blue changed lighting.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.150
After Thomas Edison invented the light bulb
in 1879, incandescent lights lit much of the

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.570
20th century.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.190
The problem is they waste a lot of energy,
lost in the form of heat, and they don’t

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.330
last long.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.860
Fluorescent lights started being used in the
1930s.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.610
Although much more efficient than incandescent,
they’re not an ideal replacement.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.540
They contain toxic mercury, age significantly
if they’re frequently switched on and off,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.770
and are prone to flicker.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.530
There needed to be a better solution.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.990
In 1961, Gary Pittman and James R. Biard of
Texas Instruments accidentally invented the

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.670
first practical light emitting diode while
trying to make a laser diode.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.630
The first LEDs emitted infrared light, invisible
to the human eye which later became useful

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.100
in things like remote controls.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.979
And for the next three decades, advances were
made to include red and green, but they couldn’t

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.150
quite get to blue - which was needed to make
white light.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:53.450
But the appeal was obvious: Unlike ordinary
incandescent bulbs, LEDs don’t have a filament

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:58.040
that will burn out, they don’t get hot,
and they require a lot less energy.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.340
So the biggest electronics companies raced
to create a powerful blue LED.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.540
But the problem of the missing color plagued
them for nearly 30 years.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.080
The key ingredient, a chemical compound called
gallium nitride proved difficult to grow in a lab.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.170
Scientists tried and failed, ultimately turning
their attention to other “more promising”

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.870
semiconductor materials for creating blue
light.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:26.060
But a number of favorable circumstances lead
a scientist from a small chemical company

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.600
in Japan called Nichia to finally make the
discovery:

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:37.020
Firstly, by virtue of having little to no budget,
scientist Shuji Nakamura was forced to create

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.200
red and infrared LEDs from scratch, using
parts he scavenged and fixed by hand.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.040
Most companies in the ‘80s were creating
LED material using commercially available equipment.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.020
This experience, which took him around 10
years and featured monthly explosions in the

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:58.080
lab, would later prove invaluable when doing
trials for blue LEDs.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.880
The second reason was his decision to use
gallium nitride, a material considered a “dead

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.250
end” by other scientists.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.870
But his motivation for using the chemical
compound was personal: getting his Ph.D.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.441
According to Nakamura, writing papers on less
promising candidates for blue light would

00:03:14.441 --> 00:03:18.970
make it much easier for him to get the necessary
papers published for his degree.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.120
Again he went back to the lab, not taking
holidays and not varying his daily routine.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.480
But this time was different.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.750
He convinced Nichia to buy the equipment for
manufacturing LED material.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.010
Instead of starting from scratch, he made
small modifications to the commercially available

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:40.620
equipment -- his extensive experience building
red and infrared LEDs aided the alterations.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.519
Just over a year later, Nakamura made his
first successful growth of gallium nitride.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.760
His method, called “two-flow MOCVD”, is
still used to this day.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.820
From this, and discoveries of other Japanese
scientists around that time, he was able to

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.330
produce the first brightly shining blue LED.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:05.460
Nichia is still a leader in the LED industry,
used by Apple and other electronics manufacturers.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:11.349
In 2014 Shuji Nakamura was awarded the Nobel
Prize in physics for his invention, along

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:16.519
with two other Japanese scientists who developed
high-quality gallium nitride materials prior

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.759
to Nakamura’s breakthrough.

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:24.309
The small, energy-efficient, and extremely
bright LEDs started a light revolution and

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:27.969
are now used in almost every piece of electronics.

00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:32.080
Without it, much of what we use today wouldn’t
be possible.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.080
It also has life-changing implications in
the developing world: With LEDs, solar panels

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.180
and small batteries are more than enough to
power the homes of the 1.2 billion people

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.680
who lack access to electricity.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.189
Most of those people are still burning wood
or gas for light which is not only inefficient,

00:04:48.189 --> 00:04:49.259
it causes pollution.

00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:54.240
It’s estimated that switching all lighting
to LEDs would reduce annual carbon dioxide

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.500
emissions by about the same amount as that
produced by three-quarters of the cars in the U.S.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.439
That’s a potentially bigger impact than
wind or solar power.

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:08.940
And with global warming due to human activity
generating catastrophic effects on the planet,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.779
the desire for saving energy is bigger than
ever.

